==English==
{{was wotd|2023|February|22}}

===Etymology===
Originally 1980s US college slang, from the notion that consumption of beer and other alcoholic beverages causes people to see things differently, as if wearing a type of goggles.<ref>Compare {{R:OED Online|subpos=n|subid=24678897|entry=beer|pos=''n.''{{sup|1}}|noformat=1|id=16986|date=September 2022}}</ref><ref>{{R:Dictionary.com|pos=plural n|source=Random House}}</ref>

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈbɪə ˌɡɒɡl̩z/|a=RP}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈbɪɚ ˌɡɑɡ(ə)l/|a=GA}}
* {{audio|en|En-au-beer goggles.ogg|a=AU}}
* {{hyphenation|en|beer gog|gles}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|p}}

# {{lb|en|idiomatic|originally|US|humorous|informal}} The [[illusion]] that [[person#Noun|people]] are more [[sexually]] [[attractive]], [[bring on|brought on]] by [[alcohol#Noun|alcohol]] [[consumption]].
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Linda Geddes|title=‘Beer goggles’ study finds alcohol does not make people seem better looking|work=The Guardian|date=2023-08-30|url=https://www.theguardian.com/society/2023/aug/30/beer-goggles-study-finds-alcohol-does-not-make-people-seem-better-looking|issn=0261-3077|passage=“Their findings essentially suggest that while intoxication may not have resulted in '''beer goggles''', it did seem to increase liquid courage, in that people were more likely to indicate a desire to interact with attractive others,” said Monk, whose previous research had found some evidence to support the '''beer goggles''' effect.}}

